Let the number of generated .c files be set independently of VJOBS.

-parallel-cc splits the generated C into util.nr_jobs files and compiles them
nr_jobs at a time, so the only way to split finely is to also run that many C
compilers at once. Splitting is needed here for a format limit, not for speed:
a 32-bit Mach-O object cannot hold a section larger than 16 MB that has
scattered relocation entries (24-bit r_address), and on ppc every reference
from code to a local symbol is one. V's own -prod text is ~22 MB in one file,
so cctools as fails with "Section too large, can't encode r_address (...)
into 24-bits of scattered relocation entry". V_PARALLEL_CC_FILES=n gives n
files while VJOBS still limits the concurrent compilers (and the memory).

--- vlib/v/builder/cbuilder/parallel_cc.v.original
+++ vlib/v/builder/cbuilder/parallel_cc.v
@@ -34,7 +34,14 @@
 	defer {
 		eprint_time(sw_total, @METHOD)
 	}
-	c_files := int_max(1, util.nr_jobs)
+	// V_PARALLEL_CC_FILES sets how many .c files the generated code is split
+	// into, independently of how many C compilers run at once (VJOBS).
+	// Stock V takes both from VJOBS, so splitting a big program finely enough
+	// for its object files to stay within a format limit (32-bit Mach-O keeps
+	// 24 bits for the address of a scattered relocation entry, so no section
+	// with such entries may exceed 16 MB) would also multiply the peak memory.
+	env_c_files := os.getenv('V_PARALLEL_CC_FILES').int()
+	c_files := if env_c_files > 0 { env_c_files } else { int_max(1, util.nr_jobs) }
 	eprintln('> c_files: ${c_files} | util.nr_jobs: ${util.nr_jobs}')
 
 	// Write generated stuff in `g.out` before and after the `out_fn_start_pos` locations,
